  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low stretching from central into\nnortheastern South Dakota, with high over eastern Montana. Upper\nlevel analysis places low off the Pacific Northwest coast, with\nridge through the Upper Midwest. Over our area, main area of\nsevere storms has now pushed into Minnesota, while some\nadditional showers and storms continue to develop over north\ncentral North Dakota.\n\nFor the rest of this afternoon, showers and some storms will\ncontinue to develop and push through northern and eastern\nportions of our area, mainly over north central North Dakota.\nWith that said, believe the severe risk has backed off for us\ngiven the widespread morning convection we had which really\n\"washed out\" the atmosphere, which has yet to recover to make\nsevere a great possibility. By this evening, precipitation \nshould be to the east of our area with a quiet night on tap.\n\nFriday will bring notably warmer temperatures to many \nlocations, especially to the south and west, with quiet weather \nexpected. With that said, south to southeasterly winds will be \non the increase as a surface low deepens over southeast Montana \ninto parts of northeast Wyoming. Notably drier air is expected \nover our far southwest resulting in low humidity values. This \nwill combine with the aforementioned winds and dry fuels to \nbring critical fire weather conditions to Slope and Bowman \nCounties, and near critical conditions to many other parts of \nsouthwest/west central North Dakota. A weak wave moving through \nthe area Saturday night may result in a few showers/storms \novernight.\n\nOn Saturday, warm airmass remains well situated over the area\nwith upper ridge centered just to our east and upper low\nsituated over the Pacific Northwest. Short waves pushing up the\nflow ahead of the low start pushing into the area starting\nprecipitation chances.\n\nOn Sunday, surface low shifts to our south/southwest with moist\neast to southeasterly flow increasing moisture over our area. A\nvariety of waves eject around the upper low bringing increasing\nchances for precipitation, particularly towards evening when\nguidance is showing widespread rain over much of the area. This\ncontinues into Monday as the low starts ejecting to the \nnortheast. Pattern then remains a bit unsettled as we approach \nmid-week with near daily low precipitation chances and \ntemperatures that are closer to average.",
